descartes-real-distinction.md:1: argue/readings: the conclusion is read 2 ways: descartes-real-distinction.logic.r3 and descartes-real-distinction.logic.r3.2 have "distinct_from_body ∧ more_easily_known ∧ persists_without_body"; descartes-real-distinction.logic.r3.3 has "essence_thinking ∧ ¬needs_place ∧ ¬depends_material"; they differ when only "I am a substance whose whole essence or nature consists only in thinking", "in order to exist I have need of some place", "I am dependent on some material thing", "the mind by which I am what I am is wholly distinct from the body", "the mind is more easily known than the body" and "although the body were not, the mind would still continue to be all that it is" are true, which the first allows
descartes-real-distinction.md:1: argue/answered: the conclusion: answered no, which keeps descartes-real-distinction.logic.r3.3, to: Suppose "I am a substance whose whole essence or nature consists only in thinking", "in order to exist I have need of some place", "I am dependent on some material thing", "the mind by which I am what I am is wholly distinct from the body", "the mind is more easily known than the body" and "although the body were not, the mind would still continue to be all that it is" are true. Is the conclusion, "In the next place, I attentively examined what I was and as I observed that I could suppose that I had no body, and that there was no world nor any place in which I might be; but that I could not therefore suppose that I was not; and that, on the contrary, from the very circumstance that I thought to doubt of the truth of other things, it most clearly and certainly followed that I was; while, on the other hand, if I had only ceased to think, although all the other objects which I had ever imagined had been in reality existent, I would have had no reason to believe that I existed; I thence concluded that I was a substance whose whole essence or nature consists only in thinking, and which, that it may exist, has need of no place, nor is dependent on any material thing; so that “I,” that is to say, the mind by which I am what I am, is wholly distinct from the body, and is even more easily known than the latter, and is such, that although the latter were not, it would still continue to be all that it is.", true then?
